{
  "abstract": "The graph polynomial (Kirchhoff polynomial, first Symanzik polynomial) of a finite multigraph is Psi = sum over spanning trees T of the product of the edge variables alpha_e for e not in T. Its zero set, the graph hypersurface, carries the Feynman periods of the graph, and Kontsevich asked whether its number of points over a finite field with q elements is always a polynomial in q. This is false in general (Belkale–Brosnan 2003; explicit counterexamples have 14 edges) but true for small graphs. This entry proves in Lean 4 and Mathlib that it holds for every series–parallel graph, together with the combinatorial rules behind it and exact counts for two families. Seven statements are compared.\n(1)-(4) Series and parallel rules. For two-terminal multigraphs, let Phi be the 2-forest polynomial (sum over spanning 2-forests separating the terminals of the product of the variables of the edges outside the forest). Under series composition Psi = Psi1 Psi2 and Phi = Phi1 Psi2 + Psi1 Phi2; under parallel composition Psi = Psi1 Phi2 + Phi1 Psi2 and Phi = Phi1 Phi2, with the two edge sets disjoint. (In the electrical reading, with alpha_e the resistance of edge e, Phi/Psi is the effective resistance between the terminals.) (5) For every series–parallel graph (built from single edges by series and parallel composition) there is a single integer polynomial P such that, for every finite field with q elements, the affine graph hypersurface {a : Psi(a) = 0} has exactly P(q) points. (6) For the banana graph with m parallel edges (Psi = e_(m-1)) the count is q^m - (q-1)^m - m(q-1)^(m-1) + ((q-1)^m + (-1)^m (q-1))/q, stated multiplied by q. (7) For K4, the smallest graph that is not series–parallel, the count is q^2 (q^3 + q - 1).\nThe proof of (5) tracks four counts per graph: the points where Psi and Phi both vanish, only Psi vanishes, only Phi vanishes, and neither vanishes. Psi and Phi are homogeneous of consecutive degrees, so scaling all variables by a nonzero constant scales the ratio Phi/Psi by that constant; hence on the last set the ratio is equidistributed over the nonzero field elements. With this the four counts of a series or parallel composition are explicit polynomial functions of the counts of the parts, and induction gives (5). The count (7) is proved by eliminating one variable at a time, using that Psi is linear in each variable.\nThe results are known in substance: polynomiality for series–parallel graphs follows from Stembridge's reductions (1998) and is stated by Brown and Schnetz (2012), citing Aluffi and Marcolli; the banana and K4 counts are classical. We did not find the (Psi, Phi) series–parallel rules or the equidistribution argument stated in this form in the literature. We searched Mathlib (source and pull requests), other Lean repositories via GitHub code search, the Isabelle AFP, Coq/Rocq, HOL, Agda, Mizar and the Palomar registry (November 2026) and found no earlier formalization of graph hypersurfaces or of Kontsevich's question; priority is nevertheless unknown.",
